WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.408
The next command we are going to explore is tracert, short for traceroute. Let me get one started and then I will explain what is going on.

00:09.408 --> 00:22.652
I will type tracert www.learnkey.com, and press Enter, and what is happening here is we are seeing each router,

00:22.652 --> 00:35.529
each hop, on the way from this device to the destination of learnkey.com. This is used to possibly troubleshoot where data is having trouble reaching a destination.

00:35.529 --> 00:47.449
Specifically, if you see a bunch of asterisks here, like we are right now, instead of return times, you can deduce that the problem is with that particular router,

00:47.449 --> 00:55.689
or it's just looking for another route. In real life, this tool is used to troubleshoot intermittent connectivity problems.

00:55.689 --> 01:04.484
And, you will be able to see if this problem is with one of your routers or not. You may have to run this several times to get a pattern.

01:04.484 --> 01:13.772
And despite a couple of issues here, the trace completed, so we see that this route is fine for now.

